The factory builds every site: sites.yml and bin/build-sites.py
Multitenancy stops being the name of a session and starts being code. Four
files, no deploys, nothing structural.

```
sites.yml               the canonical list
bin/build-sites.py      builds each site independently
bin/test_build_sites.py 17 tests
docs/TENANCY.md         two of its stated constraints retired, marked in place
```

## The restructure did most of the work already

When the build root moved into `site/`, that directory became a **self-contained
Jekyll project** — its own `_config.yml`, `Gemfile`, `.ruby-version`,
`wrangler.jsonc`. `site/` stopped being *the* site and became *a* site, and
`site-template/` is already another one beside it. **A site is a directory with
a `_config.yml` in it**, and there is no longer anything to special-case.

So the factory had two real inputs before it had a factory, and this PR is
mostly wiring rather than invention.

## `sites.yml` — one list, four roles

The gitlink says where a site's repository is; this says what we do about it.
Both committed, which is what *"a canonical list, and it is backed in by the
repository"* buys: the list cannot drift from what was reviewed, and delisting
somebody is a reviewable act.

| role | built | published | failing it is ours |
|---|---|---|---|
| `site` | yes | yes | **yes** |
| `scaffold` | yes | no | **yes** |
| `tenant` | yes | yes | no |
| `listed` | never | never | — |

**One list rather than three**, because a site we publish, a site somebody
ejected, and a producer who never started with us are the same kind of thing — a
pointer with a policy. Three registries would have needed a fourth for the case
with no ejection event to key off.

## The cadence promise, in code

*"We promise a cadence that we can meet instead of real time, which is the crux
of it all."* But *"building kinda all at once"* puts one broken tenant in the
same run as every healthy one — so:

- every site is built **independently**, and the run continues past a failure;
- a `tenant` failure is reported and **does not** make the run non-zero, because
  it is not a defect in this repository;
- `--strict` reads it the other way when you want the harsher check;
- an unhydrated tenant is **`absent`, not a failure** — a mostly-unhydrated list
  is the normal resting state, per *"they don't all have to be there"*.

There is a test that asserts the run attempts `first`, `broken`, `last` in order
and that none of it is fatal, because that ordering *is* the promise.

Also refuses a zero exit with no `index.html`: deploying that replaces a working
site with an empty one and reports success.

## What I measured, and it retires two documented costs

**With an explicit `--source`, Jekyll reads `<source>/_config.yml` — not one in
the working directory.** Verified by building `site-template/` from `site/`'s
bundle and getting a page titled *"Your Show"*.

So **one toolchain builds every site**: one Ruby, one Gemfile, one lockfile, all
at the host. That is *"defaults live in the host, not in the tenant"* arriving as
plumbing, and it means **a member's repository needs no Gemfile, no lockfile and
no Ruby pin to be built here.**

`TENANCY.md` listed four costs for a second site. Two are now gone — a second
site needs no `-c`, and `_site` is already per-site — and I marked them
superseded in place rather than quietly editing, with the measurement. The
config generator from item 1 is still wanted; it is just only that now.

**This is also a data point for the prune question**, though not an answer: the
managed build does not need the tenant's `Gemfile`. Whether it should go anyway
still depends on your call about whether ejection injects files back, which is
still open and still where the pruner is blocked.

## What it deliberately does not do

**It does not deploy.** Which Cloudflare account a tenant lands in is decided in
prose and not in configuration — your constellation by default, FCPM's account
once a member buys a domain. Building is the half that is fully specified, so it
is the half that is built.

**No workflow.** You had me remove a deploy workflow two days ago to stop two
paths being confusable, and adding a scheduled one in the same week wants your
say-so rather than my initiative. The cadence run is `python3
bin/build-sites.py` and it is ready to be put on a schedule the moment you want
one — that is the obvious next commit, not a missing piece.

**It does not touch `site-template/`'s contents**, so it cannot collide with the
pruning work.

## Verified

- `python3 bin/test_build_sites.py` — **17 tests, OK**, including a real build of
  both `site/` and `site-template/`.
- `python3 site/bin/test_no_secrets.py` — 12 tests, OK (2 skipped).
- `--only` builds one; `--only <unknown>` exits 2 with a named error; `--list`
  builds nothing.
- Build output lands in each site's `_site`, already gitignored at any depth;
  the tree is clean after a full run.

## One environment note

`bin/build-sites.py` needs PyYAML, guarded with the same message
`site/bin/sync-feeds.py` uses. On this machine the bare `python3` does not have
it — `uv run --with pyyaml python bin/build-sites.py` is how I ran it. Worth
knowing before it goes in a workflow, where it is one `pip install` line like
the existing syncs.

